.TH TIMBERGRAPH 1 "2026-09-06" "timberfs" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
timbergraph \- graph a timberfs tally store
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B timbergraph
.RB [ \-m
.IR METRIC ]
.RB [ \-\-by
.IR LABEL ]
.RI [ FILE .\|.\|.]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Draws what a metric did over time, from
.BR timberfs (1)
tally lines on stdin or in a file. ⚠ EXPERIMENTAL: the flags and the
output may move.
.PP
.nf
.RS
timberfs query app\-tally \-\-from 13:00 | timbergraph \-m http_requests \-\-by status
.RE
.fi
.PP
A tally line is text, so this needs nothing of timberfs beyond a store to
read. With no
.B \-m
it says what the input holds and draws nothing.
.PP
.B WHAT IT KNOWS WITHOUT BEING TOLD
.PP
Most of what a plot must know is in the line, because the measure names
ARE the aggregation rules:
.B count
and
.B sum
are additive, so a series may be summed over labels you did not ask to
see;
.B min
and
.B max
take the extreme;
.B last
is a gauge and is
.I never
summed across series \(em a plot that collapsed one would be inventing a
number, so it is refused rather than drawn. An
.B le
label makes a CUMULATIVE ladder, which is the one a grapher gets WRONG
rather than ugly: stacking those multiplies the count, so drawing one
without
.B \-\-quantile
or
.B "\-\-by le"
is refused too.
.PP
⚠ What a line cannot say is the UNIT, and which metrics exist but were
silent in this window. A
.B !meta
marker supplies the unit where the window happens to hold one; otherwise
name the extractor document with
.BR \-\-using .
.PP
.B THE MARKERS ARE THE ERROR BARS
.PP
.B !drop
says the numbers in that bucket are WRONG \(em a line the metric claimed
and could not read.
.B !cap
says they are understated,
.B !late
that they hold entries displaced from another bucket, and
.B !gap
that the follower could not see the window at all. They are reported on
stderr and drawn as marks in an image. A plot that hid them would be
lying about being exact.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-m " METRIC" ", " \-\-metric " METRIC"
Which metric to draw. Repeatable, or comma-separated, to put several on
one graph. Omitted, the input is summarised instead.
.PP
.RS
⚠ The UNIT decides the y axis, and it alone \(em it is what makes two
numbers comparable. Two response times share one; a request count and a
byte total do not, so the second goes on the right and the legend says
so. Drawing them as though they shared a scale is how a plot makes any
two things look related, which matters most in the case somebody plots
two metrics FOR. A third unit is refused: nobody can read three y axes.
.RE
.TP
.BI \-\-by " LABEL"
One line per value of this label, summed over the rest. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-where " K=V"
Only series carrying this label. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-field " NAME"
Which measure: count, sum, min, max or last. The default prefers
.B sum
over
.BR count ,
a metric that states both being one that measures what it sums.
.TP
.B \-\-rate
Divide by the bucket width: per second. Refused on a measure that is not
a total.
.TP
.BI \-\-against " METRIC"
A SCATTER: one point per bucket, x this metric and y the one named by
.BR \-m .
⚠ THE HONEST PLOT FOR "ARE THESE TWO RELATED". Two lines on a shared
time axis \(em especially on two y scales \(em is the picture that
INVITES seeing a relationship that is not there, since almost any pair
can be made to look correlated by choosing the scales. A cloud stays a
cloud, and the SHAPE of a real relationship is visible where two time
lines never show it. In an image, time becomes the colour, so a
relationship that drifted looks different from one that held.
.PP
.RS
Strict about three things, all because a point that is not a pair is not
data: the buckets must be the same WIDTH; each side must resolve to one
series or pair on labels named with
.BR \-\-by ;
and a bucket present on one side only is DROPPED AND COUNTED, never
quietly halved.
.RE
.TP
.BI \-\-quantile " Q"
Of a histogram's cumulative ladder, interpolated inside the bucket it
falls in \(em which is what additive data supports, and the reason a
stored percentile was never allowed.
.TP
.BI \-\-using " PATH"
An extractor document, for the unit and the title. ⚠ Named here and
nowhere else: recording it in the tally store would have to be kept in
step with the provisioning that applies it, and would travel to hosts
where the name resolves to a different document or to none.
.TP
.B \-\-list
Say what the input holds and draw nothing.
.TP
.BI \-\-png " FILE" ", " \-\-svg " FILE" ", " \-\-window
Draw somewhere other than the terminal.
.B \-\-window
needs a display and gnuplot's qt or x11 terminal.
.TP
.BI \-\-tsv " FILE" ", " \-\-gnuplot " FILE"
The table behind the picture, and the script that drew it with its data
inline so it re-runs alone
.RB ( "gnuplot \-p FILE" ).
⚠ Worth keeping: the expensive half is the FETCH, and it should not be
paid twice to redraw the same window a different way.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Needs
.BR gnuplot (1),
which is a SUGGESTED dependency rather than a required one \(em these
tools carry no third-party Python imports and nothing here should be the
first.
.B gnuplot\-nox
draws files;
.B gnuplot\-qt
or
.B gnuplot\-x11
is needed for
.BR \-\-window .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
drawn,
.B 2
a refusal \(em including the two it will not draw because drawing them
would be a lie.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR timberfs (1)
section
.BR tally ,
.BR timbersh (1),
.BR timberview (1),
.BR gnuplot (1)
